The CYP2C19*2 allele is one of the most extensively studied loss-of-function variants of CYP2C19.

## 3. Molecular Mechanism

The CYP2C19*2 variant involves a G-to-A nucleotide substitution at position 681 (c.681G>A).

Although this substitution is synonymous at the protein-coding level, meaning that it does not directly change the encoded amino acid, it has an important effect on RNA processing.

The nucleotide substitution disrupts normal pre-mRNA splicing by creating an aberrant splice site. This results in abnormal processing of the CYP2C19 transcript.

The abnormal transcript contains a 40-base-pair deletion involving exon 5. This deletion disrupts the normal reading frame and introduces a premature termination signal, resulting in the production of a truncated, non-functional CYP2C19 protein.

Consequently, the CYP2C19*2 allele is classified as a no-function allele.

### Molecular Consequences

1. A G-to-A substitution occurs at nucleotide position 681.
2. The variant alters normal pre-mRNA splicing.
3. An aberrant transcript containing a 40-base-pair deletion is produced.
4. The reading frame is disrupted.
5. A premature termination signal results in a truncated protein.
6. Functional CYP2C19 enzyme activity is lost.

